##############################################################################

=== WHAT THIS PROVES (the QA failure-path requirement) ===
An out-of-bounds / too-large block launch returns a HIP error that the runtime
CATCHES and REPORTS — it never silently writes a wrong result and never crashes.
This is the negative half of the Todo 20 acceptance: error surfacing must be proven,
not just the happy path.

=== THE INVALID LAUNCH ===
hip_run_main.cpp run_neg() allocates a 16-element device buffer, then launches a
probe kernel with an INTENTIONALLY INVALID block dim:

    neg_probe_kernel<<<dim3(1), dim3(2048), 0, nullptr>>>(d);   // 2048 > gfx1101 max 1024

The launch is asynchronous, so the error surfaces at hipGetLastError() /
hipDeviceSynchronize(). run_neg() uses RAW (non-aborting) HIP calls here on purpose,
so it can OBSERVE the error code rather than exit on it: it probes hipGetLastError()
via runtime::ProbeLaunchError(...), then calls hipDeviceSynchronize(), and PASSES
only if at least one of them is a real error. It then exits 1 (the test asserts the
error path is reached) and writes NO output file.

=== COMMAND ===
nix develop --impure --accept-flake-config -c ./build/hip_run/hip_run neg

=== OUTPUT (stderr, verbatim; exit code 1) ===
[polykernel-hip] neg out-of-bounds block (2048 > 1024): caught launch error: invalid configuration argument
[polykernel-hip] neg: launch-config error = invalid configuration argument
[polykernel-hip] neg: sync error          = no error
[polykernel-hip] neg: PASS - invalid launch CAUGHT + reported; no output written, no crash

exit code: 1

=== READBACK ===
- hipGetLastError() (via ProbeLaunchError) returned hipErrorInvalidConfiguration
  ("invalid configuration argument") — the too-large block WAS caught at the launch
  probe, and the message was printed with the HIP error string (no silent continue).
- hipDeviceSynchronize() returned "no error": the sticky launch error was already
  consumed by hipGetLastError(), exactly as the HIP/CUDA error model specifies. The
  runtime treats EITHER a non-success probe OR a non-success sync as a caught error,
  so this is the correct, non-silent outcome (not a miss).
- Non-zero exit (1); NO output .npy written; NO crash/abort/segfault. Reproduced
  identically on a second run (exit 1).

=== PYTEST (the negative test asserts exactly this contract) ===
nix develop --impure --accept-flake-config -c \
  python3 -m pytest tests/kernels/test_hip_run.py::test_hip_negative_launch_caught -q
.                                                                        [100%]
1 passed in 13.23s

test_hip_negative_launch_caught asserts: returncode == 1, "caught launch error" in
stderr, "invalid configuration argument" in stderr, "PASS - invalid launch CAUGHT"
in stderr. All hold. The runtime's error discipline (PK_HIP_CHECK on every real call
+ ProbeLaunchError on the intentional bad launch) is proven to CATCH a bad launch.
